FCFS (First Come First Serve) – Disk Scheduling

Definition
FCFS is a disk scheduling algorithm.
Requests are serviced in the order in which they arrive.
It follows the FIFO (First-In-First-Out) principle.

Characteristics 
Simple and easy to implement: No complex logic is required.
Processes requests in arrival order: No reordering of disk requests.
No starvation: Every request gets a chance to execute.
High seek time: Disk head may move long distances unnecessarily.
Poor performance: Not efficient for systems with many requests.

Working Principle 
Disk requests are stored in a queue.
Head starts from initial position.
Requests are serviced one by one in order.
Head moves sequentially to each request.
Total seek time is calculated.

SSTF (Shortest Seek Time First)

Definition
SSTF is a disk scheduling algorithm.
It selects the request that is closest to the current head position.
It minimizes seek time.

Characteristics 
Selects nearest request: Chooses the request with minimum head movement.
Improved performance: Reduces total seek time compared to FCFS.
May cause starvation: Requests far from head may never be serviced.
Dynamic decision making: Depends on current head position.
More efficient than FCFS: Better average seek time.

Working Principle 
Start from initial head position.
Find the closest request to current position.
Move head to that request.
Remove it from queue.
Repeat until all requests are served.
